0000                             ; =============================================================================
0000                             ;  B8Script interpreter for the 6502 (NMOS opcodes only), with the data stack
0000                             ;  in zero page
0000                             ;  Spec: docs/b8script.md (2026-11-23). -n? does no more editing than
0000                             ;        Backspace. Ctrl-C stops every build (see the console layer); the
0000                             ;        break-off cell is BRKOFF, $047B.
0000                             ; 
0000                             ;  This is the smaller of the two 6502 interpreters. b8s-6502.asm keeps the
0000                             ;  data stack in pages 2 and 3 (252 cells) and leaves zero page almost free;
0000                             ;  this one keeps it in zero page (112 cells), where the 6502's short
0000                             ;  addressing modes make nearly every handler smaller. The language is the
0000                             ;  same; the memory map and the machine-code contract differ.
0000                             ; 
0000                             ;  One switch, set in the EQU line below:
0000                             ;    CHECKED  0 = no checks: whatever the spec calls unsafe is not caught
0000                             ;             1 = a checked build, as in the spec's "Errors and checked
0000                             ;                 builds": at startup every word cell points at 0000h (see
0000                             ;                 e_undef) and the variables and the interpreter's cells are
0000                             ;                 0. It stops with S s R r (stack over/underflow), H (heap),
0000                             ;                 O (a store outside the program's space), U (undefined
0000                             ;                 word), Q (no closing quote), W (bad character after [), X
0000                             ;                 (a reserved byte), Z (divide by zero), N (brackets) or B
0000                             ;                 (Ctrl-C), printing the spec's four-line report:
0000                             ;                   Err Z @ 3,4
0000                             ;                   0/ "no"$
0000                             ;                   Stk 2: 5 0
0000                             ;                   Rtn 0
0000                             ;                 and then goes to EXIT.
0000                             ; 
0000                             ;  Assembler: asm80, C6502 mode.
0000                             ; =============================================================================
0000                CHECKED:   EQU   0   ; 1 = checked build
0000                ANSI:     EQU   1   ; 1 = the console codes send ANSI sequences;
0000                             ; 0 = they call CONAT, CONCLS, CONEOL, CONCUR
0000                             ; -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
0000                             ;  Registers and zero page
0000                             ; 
0000                             ;    IP      zero-page pointer to the next program character (16 bits)
0000                             ;    X       the data-stack pointer. Every cell is two bytes in zero page, low
0000                             ;            byte first: the top cell (TOS) is 0,X and 1,X, the one under it
0000                             ;            (NOS) 2,X and 3,X. The stack grows down: a push is DEX DEX, a
0000                             ;            drop INX INX. A cell that holds an address is used where it is,
0000                             ;            with (0,X): nothing is copied to a pointer first.
0000                             ;    $FE,X   the two bytes under the top cell (zero-page indexing wraps, so
0000                             ;    $FF,X   this is -2,X and -1,X): free memory, used as scratch.
0000                             ;    S       the hardware stack is the RETURN stack: a word call or "(" pushes
0000                             ;            the reading position (high byte first, then low) and "]" or ")"
0000                             ;            pulls it. 100 entries (200 bytes) fit with room to spare.
0000                             ;    Y, A    scratch. Y is 0 after peek/getc.
0000                             ; 
0000                             ;  Dispatch: step is called with JSR, so a handler ends with a plain RTS and
0000                             ;  lands on "JMP next" in the loop. Handlers that use the return stack ( ( ) \
0000                             ;  ] 00bbIA== and word calls ) first pull the 2-byte JSR frame off (PLA PLA) and end
0000                             ;  with JMP next instead.
0000                             ; 
0000                             ;  PTAB holds one byte per punctuation character: the low byte of its
0000                             ;  handler's address, less 1 (the dispatcher pushes an address and "returns"
0000                             ;  to it). The high byte comes from the character: the handlers of the first
0000                             ;  N1 characters start in the image's first page, those of the characters from
0000                             ;  N1 up to N2 in its second, and the rest in its third. The layout below
0000                             ;  keeps to that, and testing/chktab6502.py checks it in every build.
0000                             ; 
0000                             ;  Every punctuation handler starts with the carry clear (the dispatcher
0000                             ;  leaves it so). Several of them count on that, where the comments say.
0000                             ; 
0000                             ;  Machine-code contract (the grave): the code at TOS is entered as a
0000                             ;  subroutine, by JMP (T0) with the loop's return address on the hardware
0000                             ;  stack, so it ends with RTS (60h, which the hex loader appends). On entry the
0000                             ;  address has been dropped and X is the data-stack pointer: TOS is 0,X (low)
0000                             ;  and 1,X (high), NOS is 2,X and 3,X. To push: DEX, DEX, then store at 0,X
0000                             ;  and 1,X. To drop: INX, INX. The code may change A and Y, and X as the
0000                             ;  stack pointer; it must keep IP ($00/$01) and leave the hardware stack
0000                             ;  balanced. Zero page $02, $03, $07 and $0A-$0D are free for its own use.
0000                             ; -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
0000                IP:       EQU   00h   ; 00-01 reading position
0000                T0:       EQU   04h   ; scratch
0000                T1:       EQU   05h   
0000                T2:       EQU   06h   
0000                D0:       EQU   08h   ; divisor (division, number printing)
0000                D1:       EQU   09h   
0000                          IF   CHECKED   
                    PLACE:    EQU   D0   ; 08-09 the address of the character being
                                 ;   run, for the error report. No handler
                                 ;   uses D0/D1 before it can stop.
                                 ; The error report runs after the program, so it may use the zero page that
                                 ; machine code has ($02, $03, $0A-$0D; but not $0A-$0D once it prints
                                 ; numbers, since a full stack and "#" reach down to them):
                    P:        EQU   02h   ; 02-03 the place, copied (D0/D1 go to "#")
                    LN:       EQU   0Ah   ; 0A-0B the line being reported
0000                          ENDIF      
0000                             ; -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
0000                             ;  Memory map
0000                             ; 
0000                             ;    0000-000F  the variables above, and scratch
0000                             ;    0010-00EF  the data stack: 112 cells. X = F0 when it is empty. (0E and
0000                             ;               0F are the scratch under the top cell when it is full.)
0000                             ;    0100-01FF  hardware stack = return stack, S starts at FF
0000                             ;    0400       LET  letter cells: A..Z at +00..+33, a..z at +40..+73 (a word
0000                             ;               cell is 64 bytes below its variable), heap pointer at +7C,
0000                             ;               the -n? line buffer at +80..+FF. The checked build's cells
0000                             ;               are in the gaps: RSV at +35, PEND at +38;
0000                             ;               and every build's: the countdown CDOWN at +79, KEYBUF (the
0000                             ;               held key) at +7A, BRKOFF (the break-off cell) at +7B.
0000                             ;    0500       PROG the program text, NUL-terminated; the heap follows
0000                             ;    E000       the interpreter (ROM)
0000                             ; 
0000                             ;  The stacks sit below the letter page, so "the program's own space" (what
0000                             ;  the checked build lets ! and . write) is the page range LETPAGE..HEAPLIM-1.
0000                             ; -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
0000                KEY:      EQU   0FF06h   ; a waiting key in A, or A = 0 if none is
0000                             ;   waiting. The Z flag must match A (it does
0000                             ;   if the routine ends with LDA / RTS).
0000                CONOUT:   EQU   0FF03h   ; print the character in A
0000                EXIT:     EQU   0FF09h   ; where to go when the program ends
0000                             ;   KEY and CONOUT must preserve X and Y.
0000                          IF   1-ANSI   
                                 ; With ANSI = 0 the console codes call these four instead of sending ANSI
                                 ; sequences. All must preserve X.
                    CONAT:    EQU   0FF0Ch   ; `@  cursor to row Y, column A, both from 0
                    CONCLS:   EQU   0FF0Fh   ; `^  clear the screen and home the cursor
                    CONEOL:   EQU   0FF12h   ; `_  clear from the cursor to the end of the line
                    CONCUR:   EQU   0FF15h   ; `?  cursor off if A = 0 (the Z flag is set
                                 ;     to match), on if not
0000                          ENDIF      
0000                DSE:      EQU   0F0h   ; X when the data stack is empty
0000                DSFULL:   EQU   10h   ; X when it is full
0000                LETPAGE:   EQU   04h   
0000                LET:      EQU   LETPAGE*256   
0000                PROG:     EQU   0500h   ; where the loader puts the program text
0000                PROG1:    EQU   PROG+1   
0000                HEAPLIM:   EQU   0E0h   ; the heap must stay below E000h (a page)
0000                HEAP:     EQU   LET+7Ch   ; the heap pointer (the heap top)
0000                KEYBUF:   EQU   LET+7Ah   ; a key held back by the break check (0: none)
0000                BRKOFF:   EQU   LET+7Bh   ; the break-off cell: a program that stores a
0000                             ;   non-zero byte here gets Ctrl-C as a key
0000                SEED:     EQU   LET+76h   ; the random seed: the cell whose address `.
0000                             ;   gives. Nothing sets it at the start but
0000                             ;   the checked build's clearing
0000                CDOWN:    EQU   LET+79h   ; the break check's countdown (see pnext)
0000                RSV:      EQU   LET+35h   ; checked: S as it was before this character
0000                PEND:     EQU   LET+38h   ; checked: the program's end, where the heap
0000                             ;   starts: it may not be released below it
0000                LINEBUF:   EQU   LET+80h   ; -n? text, ending in CR
0000                RSLIM:    EQU   20h   ; lowest S allowed (checked build)
0000                MSLOOP:   EQU   40   ; n? inner loop count for about 1 ms; tune it
0000                             ;   (each pass calls KEY once; the wait for a
0000                             ;   second Ctrl-C uses it too: 250 ms)
0000                CWAIT:    EQU   250*MSLOOP/256   ; (that wait, in 256s)
0000                BASE:     EQU   0E000h   ; where the image starts: a multiple of 512
0000                HALFPG:   EQU   BASE/512   ; (the dispatcher doubles this to get a page)
0000                N1:       EQU   8   ; ! " # $ % & ' (  start in the first page,
0000                N2:       EQU   21   ; ) * + , - . / : ; < = > ?  in the second,
0000                             ; @ [ \ ] ^ _ ` 00bbIHwg ~  in the third
0000                             ; udiv: unsigned TOS / D (D <= 8000h). TOS becomes the quotient; the
0000                             ; remainder is left in the scratch under it, at $FE,X and $FF,X.
0000                             ; Dividing by 2 is one shift (the spec wants that fast).
0000                             ; (A macro, from when the two builds needed it in different places.)
0000                .macro UDIV,
0000                             ; 
0000                udiv:   LDA     #0
0000                 STA     0FEh,X
0000                 STA     0FFh,X
0000                 LDA     D0
0000                 EOR     #2
0000                 ORA     D1
0000                 BNE     ud0
0000                 LSR     1,X
0000                 ROR     0,X             ; the quotient
0000                 ROL     0FEh,X          ; the remainder: the bit that fell out
0000                 RTS
0000                ud0:    LDY     #16
0000                ud1:    ASL     0,X
0000                 ROL     1,X
0000                 ROL     0FEh,X
0000                 ROL     0FFh,X
0000                 SEC
0000                 LDA     0FEh,X
0000                 SBC     D0
0000                 STA     T0
0000                 LDA     0FFh,X
0000                 SBC     D1
0000                 BCC     ud2
0000                 STA     0FFh,X
0000                 LDA     T0
0000                 STA     0FEh,X
0000                 INC     0,X             ; the quotient bit (bit 0 is free)
0000                ud2:    DEY
0000                 BNE     ud1
0000                 RTS
0000                .endm
0000                 
E000                          .ORG   BASE   
E000                             ; =============================================================================
E000                             ;  Page 1 of 3. Startup: find the NUL, the heap starts after it
E000                             ; =============================================================================
E000   A2 FF        START:    LDX   #0FFh   
E002   9A                     TXS      ; return stack empty
E003                          IF   CHECKED   
                                 ; The first half of the letter page is zeroed: every variable, every
                                 ; cell of the interpreter's own (no key held back), and every word
                                 ; cell, which then points at 0000h: calling an undefined word stops
                                 ; with U (see e_undef).
                              INX      
                              TXA      ; A = 0
                              LDY   #7Fh   
                    STZ:      STA   LET,Y   
                              DEY      
                              BPL   stz   
E003                          ENDIF      
E003   20 42 E5               JSR   rstip   
E006   20 31 E5     ST1:      JSR   getc   
E009   D0 FB                  BNE   st1   ; IP ends just past the NUL
E00B                          IF   1-CHECKED   
E00B   8D 7A 04               STA   KEYBUF   ; (A = 0) no key held, the break key on
E00E   8D 7B 04               STA   BRKOFF   
E011                          ENDIF      
E011   A5 00                  LDA   IP   
E013   8D 7C 04               STA   HEAP   
E016                          IF   CHECKED   
                              STA   PEND   
E016                          ENDIF      
E016   A5 01                  LDA   IP+1   
E018   8D 7D 04               STA   HEAP+1   
E01B                          IF   CHECKED   
                              STA   PEND+1   
E01B                          ENDIF      
E01B   20 42 E5               JSR   rstip   
E01E   A2 F0                  LDX   #DSE   
E020                             ; ---- the inner loop (the checked build's is further down, out of this page)
E020                          IF   1-CHECKED   
E020                             ; -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
E020                             ;  The break key. A running program reads the keyboard only through getk
E020                             ;  (for "?") and pnext (the break check), which between them give the break
E020                             ;  key its rules:
E020                             ;    - Ctrl-C (03h) stops the program: silently to EXIT in the unchecked
E020                             ;      build, with the report (B) in the checked one;
E020                             ;    - any other key that the break check reads is held in KEYBUF for the
E020                             ;      next "?", and a newer one takes its place;
E020                             ;    - "?" (getk) takes the held key first;
E020                             ;    - a program that has stored a non-zero byte at BRKOFF gets Ctrl-C as a
E020                             ;      key like any other, but two of them within about 250 ms still stop it.
E020                             ;  KEY is the host's: a waiting key in A, or A = 0 with the zero flag set.
E020                             ; -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
E020                             ; pnext: the break check, then (falling through) next. A word call, a true
E020                             ; "(" and a true ")" end here, but the keyboard is looked at only once in 256
E020                             ; of them: CDOWN counts them down. A key found is held; a Ctrl-C goes on to ctlc. (Startup
E020                             ; falls in here too, which does no harm.)
E020   CE 79 04     PNEXT:    DEC   CDOWN   
E023   D0 0B                  BNE   next   
E025   20 06 FF               JSR   KEY   
E028   F0 06                  BEQ   next   ; no key
E02A   8D 7A 04               STA   KEYBUF   ; held for the next "?"
E02D   20 3C E4               JSR   chk3   ; (Ctrl-C: back only if the break key is off)
E030   20 63 E4     NEXT:     JSR   step   
E033   4C 30 E0               JMP   next   
E036                          ENDIF      
E036                          IF   CHECKED   
                              JMP   next   
E036                          ENDIF      
E036                             ; ---- handlers of ! " # $ % & ' ( : they must start in this page -------------
E036                             ; "$" prints the string at TOS, decoding escapes: "/" plus a character
E036                             ; clears that character"s bit 6 (/M is CR, /` is space; "/" and ""' have no
E036                             ; bit 6, so // and /" give themselves).
E036                             ; The checked build walks a copy of TOS along the string, so that the address
E036                             ; is still there if it stops with Q on a NUL.
E036                H_PRINT:      
E036                          IF   CHECKED   
                              JSR   h_dup   ; the copy
                              JSR   pr0   
                              BNE   pdrop   ; always (X is never 0): drop the address
                    E_QPR:    INX      
                              INX      ; the copy goes: the address is TOS again
                    E_QUOTE:   LDA   #"Q"   ; $ or a string from 22h ran into the end
                              JMP   error   
                    PR0:         
E036                          ENDIF      
E036   20 8A E2               JSR   tosget   
E039   C9 22                  CMP   #22h   
E03B   F0 28                  BEQ   pdrop   
E03D                          IF   CHECKED   
                              TAY      ; (Y is free here)
                              BEQ   e_qpr   
E03D                          ENDIF      
E03D   C9 2F                  CMP   #"/"   
E03F   D0 05                  BNE   pr1   
E041   20 8A E2               JSR   tosget   
E044   29 BF                  AND   #0BFh   
E046   20 03 FF     PR1:      JSR   CONOUT   
E049                          IF   1-CHECKED   
E049   4C 36 E0               JMP   h_print   
E04C                          ENDIF      
E04C                          IF   CHECKED   
                              JMP   pr0   
E04C                          ENDIF      
E04C                             ; 22h pushes the address of the text after it and skips the string.
E04C   20 AC E4     H_STR:    JSR   puship   
E04F                          IF   CHECKED   
                              JSR   skipstr   
                              BEQ   e_quote   ; it ran into the end: the place is the 22h
                              RTS      
E04F                          ENDIF      
E04F                             ; skipstr: IP is inside a string; step past its closing quote. "/" escapes
E04F                             ; the next character, so "/"" does not end it.
E04F                             ; Unchecked, it returns with Z set and A = 22h, and it does not look for the
E04F                             ; end. Checked, it returns with Z clear and A = 11h, or with Z set if the
E04F                             ; text ended first (a NUL, also one just after "/").
E04F                          IF   1-CHECKED   
E04F   20 31 E5     SKIPSTR:   JSR   getc   
E052   C9 2F                  CMP   #"/"   
E054   F0 05                  BEQ   ss0   
E056   C9 22                  CMP   #22h   
E058   D0 F5                  BNE   skipstr   
E05A   60                     RTS      
E05B   20 34 E5     SS0:      JSR   incip   
E05E   D0 EF                  BNE   skipstr   ; A is "/" here
E060                          ENDIF      
E060                          IF   CHECKED   
                    SKIPSTR:   JSR   getc   
                              BEQ   ss9   ; the end
                              CMP   #"/"   
                              BNE   ss1   
                              JSR   getc   ; the escaped character
                              BNE   skipstr   
                    SS9:      RTS      ; the end, just after "/"
                    SS1:      CMP   #22h   
                              BNE   skipstr   
                              LSR      ; A = 11h, Z clear
                              RTS      
E060                          ENDIF      
E060   B5 00        H_EMIT:   LDA   0,X   ; "'"
E062   20 03 FF               JSR   CONOUT   
E065   E8           PDROP:    INX      ; a drop for this page's handlers
E066   E8                     INX      
E067   60                     RTS      
E068                             ; "#" prints TOS as a signed decimal number, with no space after it. The
E068                             ; digits are divided off and held on the hardware stack, ended by a 0. udiv
E068                             ; leaves each remainder in the scratch under the top cell.
E068   B5 01        H_NUM:    LDA   1,X   
E06A   10 08                  BPL   pn1   
E06C   A9 2D                  LDA   #"-"   
E06E   20 03 FF               JSR   CONOUT   
E071   20 08 E1               JSR   neg   
E074   A9 0A        PN1:      LDA   #10   
E076   85 08                  STA   D0   
E078   A9 00                  LDA   #0   
E07A   85 09                  STA   D1   
E07C   48                     PHA      ; the end marker
E07D   20 E6 E2     PN2:      JSR   udiv   
E080   B5 FE                  LDA   0FEh,X   
E082   09 30                  ORA   #"0"   
E084   48                     PHA      
E085   B5 00                  LDA   0,X   
E087   15 01                  ORA   1,X   
E089   D0 F2                  BNE   pn2   
E08B   68           PN3:      PLA      
E08C   F0 D7                  BEQ   pdrop   
E08E   20 03 FF               JSR   CONOUT   
E091   4C 8B E0               JMP   pn3   
E094                             ; "!" ( n addr -- ): the low byte, then "."s ending for the high byte.
E094   B5 02        H_STORE:   LDA   2,X   
E096                          IF   CHECKED   
                              JSR   chkst   
E096                          ENDIF      
E096   81 00                  STA   (0,X)   
E098   20 8C E2               JSR   inctos   
E09B   B5 03                  LDA   3,X   
E09D   4C 1F E1               JMP   st9   
E0A0   B5 00        H_SWAP:   LDA   0,X   ; "%"
E0A2   B4 02                  LDY   2,X   
E0A4   94 00                  STY   0,X   
E0A6   95 02                  STA   2,X   
E0A8   B5 01                  LDA   1,X   
E0AA   B4 03                  LDY   3,X   
E0AC   94 01                  STY   1,X   
E0AE   95 03                  STA   3,X   
E0B0   60                     RTS      
E0B1                             ; "&", and "|" and "^" further down: one byte of the result goes into NOS,
E0B1                             ; and X moves up one. Done twice, that is both bytes, and NOS has become TOS.
E0B1   20 B4 E0     H_AND:    JSR   an1   
E0B4   B5 00        AN1:      LDA   0,X   
E0B6   35 02                  AND   2,X   
E0B8   95 02                  STA   2,X   
E0BA   E8                     INX      
E0BB   60                     RTS      
E0BC                             ; "(" ( flag -- ): true pushes the block start and enters; false skips to
E0BC                             ; the matching ")".
E0BC   20 BC E4     H_OPEN:   JSR   flag   ; (keeps the carry clear)
E0BF                          IF   1-CHECKED   
E0BF   F0 17                  BEQ   skip   
E0C1                          ENDIF      
E0C1                          IF   CHECKED   
                              BEQ   skpar   
E0C1                          ENDIF      
E0C1                             ; pushn: take the step frame off, push the reading position on the return
E0C1                             ; stack, and go on. With the carry set (a word call) the reading position
E0C1                             ; then becomes the address in the letter cell at LET,Y.
E0C1   68           PUSHN:    PLA      
E0C2   68                     PLA      ; drop the step frame
E0C3   A5 01                  LDA   IP+1   
E0C5   48                     PHA      
E0C6   A5 00                  LDA   IP   
E0C8   48                     PHA      
E0C9   90 0A                  BCC   nxt   
E0CB   B9 00 04               LDA   LET,Y   
E0CE   85 00                  STA   IP   
E0D0   B9 01 04               LDA   LET+1,Y   
E0D3   85 01                  STA   IP+1   
E0D5   4C 20 E0     NXT:      JMP   pnext   
E0D8                             ; ---- no handler starts between here and the next heading: these routines
E0D8                             ; ---- fill the space up to the page boundary and past it ---------------------
E0D8                             ; skip: read past text to the closer at this level, and return it in A.
E0D8                             ; Brackets of both kinds count together; strings are stepped over whole. A
E0D8                             ; NUL first ends the program. (The checked build's skip is further down.)
E0D8                          IF   1-CHECKED   
E0D8   A9 00        SKIP:     LDA   #0   
E0DA   85 06                  STA   T2   ; depth
E0DC   20 31 E5     SK1:      JSR   getc   
E0DF   F0 20                  BEQ   endprog   
E0E1   C9 22                  CMP   #22h   
E0E3   D0 03                  BNE   sk2   
E0E5   20 4F E0               JSR   skipstr   ; leaves A = 22h, which matches nothing below
E0E8   C9 28        SK2:      CMP   #"("   
E0EA   F0 11                  BEQ   sk3   
E0EC   C9 5B                  CMP   #"["   
E0EE   F0 0D                  BEQ   sk3   
E0F0   C9 29                  CMP   #")"   
E0F2   F0 04                  BEQ   sk4   
E0F4   C9 5D                  CMP   #"]"   
E0F6   D0 E4                  BNE   sk1   
E0F8   C6 06        SK4:      DEC   T2   
E0FA   10 E0                  BPL   sk1   
E0FC   60                     RTS      ; the closer at this level
E0FD   E6 06        SK3:      INC   T2   
E0FF   D0 DB                  BNE   sk1   
E101   4C 09 FF     ENDPROG:   JMP   EXIT   
E104                          ENDIF      
E104                          IF   CHECKED   
                                 ; A false ( skips: the wrong closer for it is ].
                    SKPAR:    LDA   #"]"   
                              JMP   skip   
E104                          ENDIF      
E104                             ; absv: TOS = |TOS|.  negm: TOS = -TOS if the N flag is set.
E104   B5 01        ABSV:     LDA   1,X   
E106   10 0D        NEGM:     BPL   ab9   
E108                             ; neg: TOS = -TOS
E108   38           NEG:      SEC      
E109   A9 00        NEG1:     LDA   #0   
E10B   F5 00                  SBC   0,X   
E10D   95 00                  STA   0,X   
E10F   A9 00                  LDA   #0   
E111   F5 01                  SBC   1,X   
E113   95 01                  STA   1,X   
E115   60           AB9:      RTS      
E116                             ; =============================================================================
E116                             ;  Page 2 of 3: handlers of ) * + , - . / : ; < = > ?
E116                             ; =============================================================================
E116   B5 00        H_DUP:    LDA   0,X   ; ":"
E118   B4 01                  LDY   1,X   
E11A   4C B5 E4               JMP   pushay   
E11D                             ; "." ( c addr -- ), and the end of "!": store A at TOS, drop both cells.
E11D   B5 02        H_CSTORE:   LDA   2,X   
E11F                ST9:         
E11F                          IF   CHECKED   
                              JSR   chkst   
E11F                          ENDIF      
E11F   81 00                  STA   (0,X)   
E121   E8                     INX      
E122   E8                     INX      
E123   E8           DROP:     INX      ; ";"
E124   E8                     INX      
E125   60                     RTS      
E126                             ; "-" has two jobs: directly before a digit it starts a negative number.
E126   20 17 E5     H_SUB:    JSR   isdig   
E129   90 0F                  BCC   negnum   
E12B   20 08 E1     SUB1:     JSR   neg   ; a - b = a + (-b)
E12E   18                     CLC      
E12F                             ; "+": one byte at a time, as the logic operators do; the carry goes across.
E12F                             ; It ends with A = the high byte of the sum.
E12F   20 32 E1     H_ADD:    JSR   ad1   
E132   B5 00        AD1:      LDA   0,X   
E134   75 02                  ADC   2,X   
E136   95 02                  STA   2,X   
E138   E8                     INX      
E139   60                     RTS      
E13A   A9 00        NEGNUM:   LDA   #0   ; a number from 0 (the digit routine's start)
E13C   20 E3 E4               JSR   digit   ; (comes back with the carry set)
E13F   B0 C7                  BCS   neg   ; always: the number, negated
E141                             ; Signed compares: a - b sets N and V; N xor V is "a < b". The carry ends up
E141                             ; clear for true, and bool turns it into -1 (true) or 0.
E141   20 A0 E0     H_GT:     JSR   h_swap   ; a > b is b < a
E144   20 4B E1     H_LT:     JSR   scmp   
E147   E8                     INX      
E148   E8                     INX      ; (keeps the carry; X is never 0)
E149   D0 15                  BNE   bool   ; always
E14B                             ; scmp: the carry clear if NOS < TOS (signed). `< and `> use it too.
E14B   B5 02        SCMP:     LDA   2,X   
E14D   D5 00                  CMP   0,X   
E14F   B5 03                  LDA   3,X   
E151   F5 01                  SBC   1,X   
E153   70 02                  BVS   lt1   
E155   49 80                  EOR   #80h   
E157   0A           LT1:      ASL      
E158   60                     RTS      
E159   20 2B E1     H_EQ:     JSR   sub1   ; a - b: zero if equal; A = its high byte
E15C   15 00                  ORA   0,X   
E15E   C9 01                  CMP   #1   ; carry clear if A = 0
E160   A9 00        BOOL:     LDA   #0   
E162   69 FF                  ADC   #0FFh   ; carry clear: FF, set: 00
E164   95 00                  STA   0,X   
E166   95 01                  STA   1,X   
E168   60                     RTS      
E169                             ; "*": the classic shift-right multiply. The multiplier is the NOS cell: it
E169                             ; leaves from the bottom while the product comes in at the top, through
E169                             ; A:T0 above it; after 16 rounds the cell holds the low 16 bits, which are
E169                             ; all that is kept. Dropping TOS (the multiplicand) leaves it on top.
E169   A0 10        H_MUL:    LDY   #16   ; the round count
E16B   A9 00                  LDA   #0   
E16D   85 04                  STA   T0   ; A:T0 = the upper half
E16F   56 03                  LSR   3,X   
E171   76 02                  ROR   2,X   ; the first multiplier bit
E173   90 0D        ML1:      BCC   ml2   
E175   85 05                  STA   T1   
E177   18                     CLC      
E178   A5 04                  LDA   T0   
E17A   75 00                  ADC   0,X   
E17C   85 04                  STA   T0   
E17E   A5 05                  LDA   T1   
E180   75 01                  ADC   1,X   ; upper half += the multiplicand
E182   6A           ML2:      ROR      
E183   66 04                  ROR   T0   
E185   76 03                  ROR   3,X   
E187   76 02                  ROR   2,X   ; everything one place right; next bit out
E189   88                     DEY      
E18A   D0 E7                  BNE   ml1   
E18C   E8                     INX      
E18D   E8                     INX      
E18E   60                     RTS      
E18F                             ; "/" ( a b -- q r ): the remainder is on top. Truncates toward zero; r takes
E18F                             ; a's sign. Divide |a| by |b|, then q gets the sign of a XOR b and r the sign
E18F                             ; of a. Unchecked, dividing by zero returns two cells of no meaning; checked,
E18F                             ; it stops with Z.
E18F                             ; b's cell is dropped once D holds |b|; udiv then leaves the remainder just
E18F                             ; where that cell was, so stepping X back down makes it the top cell.
E18F                          IF   CHECKED   
                    E_ZERO:   LDA   #"Z"   ; divide by zero, the stack as it was
                              JMP   error   
E18F                          ENDIF      
E18F                H_DIV:       
E18F                          IF   CHECKED   
                              LDA   0,X   
                              ORA   1,X   
                              BEQ   e_zero   
E18F                          ENDIF      
E18F   B5 03                  LDA   3,X   
E191   48                     PHA      ; a's sign: for r
E192   55 01                  EOR   1,X   
E194   48                     PHA      ; a xor b: for q
E195   20 04 E1               JSR   absv   
E198   B5 00                  LDA   0,X   
E19A   85 08                  STA   D0   
E19C   B5 01                  LDA   1,X   
E19E   85 09                  STA   D1   ; D = |b|
E1A0   E8                     INX      
E1A1   E8                     INX      
E1A2   20 04 E1               JSR   absv   ; TOS = |a|
E1A5   20 E6 E2               JSR   udiv   ; TOS = q
E1A8   68                     PLA      
E1A9   20 06 E1               JSR   negm   ; q's sign
E1AC   CA                     DEX      
E1AD   CA                     DEX      ; r on top
E1AE   68                     PLA      
E1AF   4C 06 E1               JMP   negm   ; r's sign
E1B2   A1 00        H_CFETCH:   LDA   (0,X)   ; ","
E1B4   95 00        SETA:     STA   0,X   ; TOS = A
E1B6   A9 00                  LDA   #0   
E1B8   95 01                  STA   1,X   
E1BA   60                     RTS      
E1BB                             ; ")" ( flag -- ): true jumps back to the block start (and keeps it); false
E1BB                             ; drops it and carries on. It uses "]"s code, entered past its SEC: with the
E1BB                             ; carry clear, as it is here, the address popped is pushed back.
E1BB   20 BC E4     H_CLOSE:   JSR   flag   
E1BE   F0 73                  BEQ   rdrop   
E1C0   D0 78                  BNE   ret1   ; always
E1C2                             ; "?" ( n -- c ) or ( -n -- addr )
E1C2                             ;   n > 0: wait about n ms for a key; 0 if none came
E1C2                             ;   n = 0: wait for a key
E1C2                             ;   n < 0: read a line of at most -n characters, with echo. The buffer
E1C2                             ;          holds 127 characters and the CR: a bigger count is taken as 127.
E1C2   B5 01        H_KEY:    LDA   1,X   
E1C4   30 26                  BMI   line   
E1C6   A0 28        KT1:      LDY   #MSLOOP   ; one millisecond, roughly
E1C8   20 2A E4     KT2:      JSR   getk   
E1CB   D0 E7                  BNE   seta   
E1CD   88                     DEY      
E1CE   D0 F8                  BNE   kt2   
E1D0   EE 76 04               INC   SEED   ; a millisecond with no key stirs the random
E1D3   D0 03                  BNE   kt5   ;   seed by one: how long a key takes to come
E1D5   EE 77 04               INC   SEED+1   ;   is the one thing here that is not the
E1D8                KT5:         ;   same every time
E1D8   B5 00                  LDA   0,X   ; count the millisecond off:
E1DA   D0 06                  BNE   kt4   
E1DC   B5 01                  LDA   1,X   
E1DE   F0 E6                  BEQ   kt1   ; 0? never counts down: it waits
E1E0   D6 01                  DEC   1,X   
E1E2   D6 00        KT4:      DEC   0,X   
E1E4   D0 E0                  BNE   kt1   
E1E6   B5 01                  LDA   1,X   
E1E8   D0 DC                  BNE   kt1   
E1EA   F0 C8                  BEQ   seta   ; always: the time ran out, A = 0
E1EC                             ; ---- no handler starts between here and the next heading --------------------
E1EC                             ; Backspace takes the last character back (and is echoed as it is), CR ends
E1EC                             ; (stored, not echoed), every other key is stored and echoed until the line
E1EC                             ; is full. Y = the length so far; T0 = the most allowed.
E1EC   20 08 E1     LINE:     JSR   neg   ; TOS = the count, and A = its high byte
E1EF   D0 04                  BNE   ln0   
E1F1   B5 00                  LDA   0,X   
E1F3   10 02                  BPL   ln1   
E1F5   A9 7F        LN0:      LDA   #127   ; more than 127 is taken as 127
E1F7   85 04        LN1:      STA   T0   
E1F9   A0 00                  LDY   #0   
E1FB   F0 03                  BEQ   ln2   ; always
E1FD   20 03 FF     LN3:      JSR   CONOUT   
E200   20 2A E4     LN2:      JSR   getk   
E203   F0 FB                  BEQ   ln2   
E205   C9 0D                  CMP   #13   
E207   F0 14                  BEQ   ln5   
E209   C9 08                  CMP   #8   
E20B   F0 0A                  BEQ   ln4   
E20D   C4 04                  CPY   T0   
E20F   B0 EF                  BCS   ln2   ; full
E211   99 80 04               STA   LINEBUF,Y   
E214   C8                     INY      
E215   D0 E6                  BNE   ln3   ; always: echo it
E217   88           LN4:      DEY      ; Backspace: one back, and echo it,
E218   10 E3                  BPL   ln3   
E21A   C8                     INY      ;   unless the line was empty
E21B   F0 E3                  BEQ   ln2   ; always
E21D   99 80 04     LN5:      STA   LINEBUF,Y   ; A = CR
E220   A9 80                  LDA   #<LINEBUF   
E222   A0 04                  LDY   #>LINEBUF   
E224   4C B7 E4               JMP   setay   ; TOS = the line's address
E227                             ; =============================================================================
E227                             ;  Page 3 of 3: handlers of @ [ \ ] ^ _ ` 00bbIHwg ~
E227                             ; =============================================================================
E227                             ; "\" ( flag -- ): true skips to the next ")" or "]" at this level; ")"
E227                             ; drops the block start, "]" returns.
E227   20 BC E4     H_BREAK:   JSR   flag   
E22A   F0 66                  BEQ   tg1   ; (an RTS)
E22C                          IF   CHECKED   
                              LDA   #0   ; \ takes either closer
E22C                          ENDIF      
E22C   20 D8 E0               JSR   skip   
E22F   C9 29                  CMP   #")"   
E231   D0 06                  BNE   h_ret   
E233   68           RDROP:    PLA      
E234   68                     PLA      
E235   68                     PLA      
E236   68                     PLA      ; (the block start's high byte: never 0)
E237   D0 28                  BNE   nxt2   ; always
E239                             ; "]" returns: pull an address and jump to it. ")" comes in at ret1 with the
E239                             ; carry clear, and then the address is pushed back.
E239   38           H_RET:    SEC      
E23A                RET1:        
E23A   68                     PLA      
E23B   68                     PLA      ; drop the step frame
E23C   68                     PLA      
E23D   85 00                  STA   IP   
E23F   68                     PLA      
E240   85 01                  STA   IP+1   
E242   B0 1D                  BCS   nxt2   
E244   48                     PHA      ; ")": keep the block start
E245   A5 00                  LDA   IP   
E247   48                     PHA      
E248   4C 20 E0               JMP   pnext   ; a loop-back: the break check
E24B                             ; "{" TOS to the return stack, "}" return stack to TOS
E24B   68           H_TOR:    PLA      
E24C   68                     PLA      
E24D   B5 01                  LDA   1,X   
E24F   48                     PHA      
E250   B5 00                  LDA   0,X   
E252   48                     PHA      
E253   E8                     INX      
E254   E8                     INX      
E255   D0 0A                  BNE   nxt2   ; always: X is never 0
E257   68           H_FROMR:   PLA      
E258   68                     PLA      
E259   CA                     DEX      
E25A   CA                     DEX      
E25B   68                     PLA      
E25C   95 00                  STA   0,X   
E25E   68                     PLA      
E25F   95 01                  STA   1,X   
E261   4C 30 E0     NXT2:     JMP   next   
E264                             ; "~": -x - 1, which is neg with the carry clear, as it is here
E264   4C 09 E1     H_NOT:    JMP   neg1   
E267   20 6A E2     H_XOR:    JSR   xr1   
E26A   B5 00        XR1:      LDA   0,X   
E26C   55 02                  EOR   2,X   
E26E   95 02                  STA   2,X   
E270   E8                     INX      
E271   60                     RTS      
E272   20 75 E2     H_OR:     JSR   or1   
E275   B5 00        OR1:      LDA   0,X   
E277   15 02                  ORA   2,X   
E279   95 02                  STA   2,X   
E27B   E8                     INX      
E27C   60                     RTS      
E27D   A1 00        H_FETCH:   LDA   (0,X)   ; "@"
E27F   A8                     TAY      
E280   20 8C E2               JSR   inctos   
E283   A1 00                  LDA   (0,X)   
E285   95 01                  STA   1,X   
E287   94 00                  STY   0,X   
E289   60                     RTS      
E28A                             ; tosget: A = the byte at TOS, and TOS moves on.  inctos: TOS moves on.
E28A   A1 00        TOSGET:   LDA   (0,X)   
E28C   F6 00        INCTOS:   INC   0,X   
E28E   D0 02                  BNE   tg1   
E290   F6 01                  INC   1,X   
E292   60           TG1:      RTS      
E293                             ; "[" followed by a letter stores the body"s address in that letter"s cell:
E293                             ; uppercase is a word, lowercase a variable. Followed by a space it pushes
E293                             ; the body"s address instead. Either way it then skips to the matching "]'.
E293                             ; (The unchecked build takes anything that is not a letter as the space; the
E293                             ; checked build stops on it.)
E293                          IF   CHECKED   
                    DEF1:     CPY   #" "-"A"+256   
                              BEQ   def2   
                                 ; W: anything but a letter or a space after [. The place is that character,
                                 ; one past the [.
                              INC   PLACE   
                              BNE   eb1   
                              INC   PLACE+1   
                    EB1:      LDA   #"W"   
                              JMP   error   
E293                          ENDIF      
E293   20 31 E5     H_DEF:    JSR   getc   
E296   38                     SEC      
E297   E9 41                  SBC   #"A"   ; a letter gives 0-25 or 32-57: lowercase
E299   A8                     TAY      ;   lands 64 bytes up
E29A   29 DF                  AND   #0DFh   ; fold case: 0-25 only for a letter
E29C   C9 1A                  CMP   #26   
E29E                          IF   1-CHECKED   
E29E   B0 0F                  BCS   def2   ; not a letter
E2A0                          ENDIF      
E2A0                          IF   CHECKED   
                              BCS   def1   
E2A0                          ENDIF      
E2A0   98                     TYA      
E2A1   0A                     ASL      
E2A2   A8                     TAY      ; Y -> the cell
E2A3   A5 00                  LDA   IP   
E2A5   99 00 04               STA   LET,Y   
E2A8   A5 01                  LDA   IP+1   
E2AA   99 01 04               STA   LET+1,Y   
E2AD   D0 03                  BNE   dsk   ; always: no program is in page zero
E2AF   20 AC E4     DEF2:     JSR   puship   ; an anonymous block: push its address
E2B2                DSK:         
E2B2                          IF   CHECKED   
                              LDA   #")"   ; the wrong closer for [
E2B2                          ENDIF      
E2B2   4C D8 E0               JMP   skip   
E2B5                             ; ---- the grave
E2B5                             ; The grave is a prefix: with the character after it, it is one command.
E2B5                             ; GCH (after the three pages, with the handlers) holds the characters and
E2B5                             ; GLO, beside it, the low byte of each one's handler, less 1 for the RTS
E2B5                             ; trick. The high byte is worked out: the handlers lie in the order of the
E2B5                             ; table and take up less than a page, so they start in one page or in two,
E2B5                             ; and GK is the place in the table where the second page begins (the
E2B5                             ; assembler works it out, and chktab6502.py checks it). A handler starts
E2B5                             ; with Y = its place in the table, and ends in RTS. A character that is not
E2B5                             ; in the table is unsafe: the unchecked build does nothing, the checked
E2B5                             ; build stops with W.
E2B5                H_GRAVE:      
E2B5                          IF   CHECKED   
                              INC   PLACE   ; the place of anything that goes wrong in
                              BNE   gr0   ;   one of these is the character after the
                              INC   PLACE+1   ;   grave
                    GR0:         
E2B5                          ENDIF      
E2B5   20 31 E5               JSR   getc   
E2B8   A0 0C                  LDY   #GLO-GCH-1   
E2BA   D9 1E E3     GR1:      CMP   GCH,Y   
E2BD   F0 04                  BEQ   gr2   
E2BF   88                     DEY      
E2C0   10 F8                  BPL   gr1   
E2C2                          IF   1-CHECKED   
E2C2   60                     RTS      ; no such code
E2C3                          ENDIF      
E2C3                          IF   CHECKED   
                              JMP   eb1   ; no such code: W
E2C3                          ENDIF      
E2C3   C0 0A        GR2:      CPY   #GK   ; carry: the second page
E2C5   A9 E3                  LDA   #GQ0   
E2C7   69 00                  ADC   #0   
E2C9   48                     PHA      
E2CA   B9 2B E3               LDA   GLO,Y   
E2CD   48                     PHA      
E2CE   60                     RTS      ; to the handler
E2CF                             ; ---- "_" is the last handler: its body may leave the page
E2CF                             ; "_" ( n -- addr ): the heap top moves by n, which is signed: a negative
E2CF                             ; count releases. TOS becomes the old top. (The carry is clear on the way in.)
E2CF                          IF   1-CHECKED   
E2CF   AD 7C 04     H_ALLOC:   LDA   HEAP   
E2D2   A8                     TAY      
E2D3   75 00                  ADC   0,X   
E2D5   8D 7C 04               STA   HEAP   
E2D8   94 00                  STY   0,X   
E2DA   AD 7D 04               LDA   HEAP+1   
E2DD   A8                     TAY      
E2DE   75 01                  ADC   1,X   
E2E0   8D 7D 04               STA   HEAP+1   
E2E3   94 01                  STY   1,X   
E2E5   60                     RTS      
E2E6                          ENDIF      
E2E6                             ; The checked build works the new top out first, in T0 and Y, and stops with
E2E6                             ; H, the count still on the stack, if it is at or past the limit, below the
E2E6                             ; program's end, or if the 16-bit add wrapped. A wrap is the V flag of the
E2E6                             ; high bytes" add with the old top"s sign bit flipped: that makes the old top
E2E6                             ; a signed number from -8000h, so the add overflows exactly when the true
E2E6                             ; sum leaves 0000h-FFFFh.
E2E6                          IF   CHECKED   
                    H_ALLOC:   LDA   HEAP   
                              ADC   0,X   
                              STA   T0   
                              LDA   HEAP+1   
                              EOR   #80h   
                              ADC   1,X   
                              BVS   e_heap   ; wrapped
                              EOR   #80h   ; the new top's high byte
                              CMP   #HEAPLIM   
                              BCS   e_heap   ; at or past the limit
                              TAY      
                              LDA   T0   
                              CMP   PEND   
                              TYA      
                              SBC   PEND+1   
                              BCC   e_heap   ; below the program's end
                              LDA   HEAP   
                              STA   0,X   
                              LDA   T0   
                              STA   HEAP   
                              LDA   HEAP+1   
                              STA   1,X   
                              STY   HEAP+1   
                              RTS      
                                 ; Errors found by handlers. The stacks are as they were before the handler.
                    E_HEAP:   LDA   #"H"   ; heap past the limit or below the program
                              DB   2Ch   ; (BIT abs: hides the next instruction)
                    E_STORE:   LDA   #"O"   ; a store outside the program's space
                              JMP   error   
                                 ; chkst: stop unless the address in the top cell is in the program's own
                                 ; space, from the letter page up to the heap limit. Keeps A and Y.
                    CHKST:    PHA      
                              LDA   1,X   
                              CMP   #LETPAGE   
                              BCC   e_store   
                              CMP   #HEAPLIM   
                              BCS   e_store   
                              PLA      
                              RTS      
E2E6                          ENDIF      
E2E6                             ; =============================================================================
E2E6                             ;  The rest: no handler starts past this point
E2E6                             ; =============================================================================
E2E6                          ;*Macro unroll:  UDIV
E2E6   A9 00        UDIV:     LDA   #0   
E2E8   95 FE                  STA   0FEh,X   
E2EA   95 FF                  STA   0FFh,X   
E2EC   A5 08                  LDA   D0   
E2EE   49 02                  EOR   #2   
E2F0   05 09                  ORA   D1   
E2F2   D0 07                  BNE   ud0   
E2F4   56 01                  LSR   1,X   
E2F6   76 00                  ROR   0,X   
E2F8   36 FE                  ROL   0FEh,X   
E2FA   60                     RTS      
E2FB   A0 10        UD0:      LDY   #16   
E2FD   16 00        UD1:      ASL   0,X   
E2FF   36 01                  ROL   1,X   
E301   36 FE                  ROL   0FEh,X   
E303   36 FF                  ROL   0FFh,X   
E305   38                     SEC      
E306   B5 FE                  LDA   0FEh,X   
E308   E5 08                  SBC   D0   
E30A   85 04                  STA   T0   
E30C   B5 FF                  LDA   0FFh,X   
E30E   E5 09                  SBC   D1   
E310   90 08                  BCC   ud2   
E312   95 FF                  STA   0FFh,X   
E314   A5 04                  LDA   T0   
E316   95 FE                  STA   0FEh,X   
E318   F6 00                  INC   0,X   
E31A   88           UD2:      DEY      
E31B   D0 E0                  BNE   ud1   
E31D   60                     RTS      
E31E                             ; =============================================================================
E31E                             ;  The grave's codes (h_grave, in the third page, is the dispatcher). Each
E31E                             ;  starts with Y = its place in the table, GCH.
E31E                             ; 
E31E                             ;    `!  ( addr -- )        call the machine code at addr
E31E                             ;    `$  ( addr -- addr' )  the hex loader: pairs of hex digits are stored
E31E                             ;                           from addr until a non-hex character; then a RTS
E31E                             ;                           (60h) is written and its address is left. An odd
E31E                             ;                           last digit is consumed and dropped.
E31E                             ;    `'  ( -- )             new line: CR LF
E31E                             ;    `+  ( addr -- n )      add 1 to the cell at addr and leave its new value
E31E                             ;    `,  ( n -- r )         a random number from 0 to n-1 (n is 1 to 32767)
E31E                             ;    `-  ( addr -- n )      subtract 1 from the cell at addr, the same way
E31E                             ;    `.  ( -- addr )        the address of the random seed
E31E                             ;    `<  ( a b -- n )       the smaller of the two (signed)
E31E                             ;    `>  ( a b -- n )       the larger
E31E                             ;    `?  ( flag -- )        the cursor off (0) or on
E31E                             ;    `@  ( row col -- )     the cursor to that row and column, both from 0
E31E                             ;    `^  ( -- )             clear the screen and home the cursor
E31E                             ;    `_  ( -- )             clear from the cursor to the end of the line
E31E                             ; =============================================================================
E31E                             ; (The order matters: "-" just before "+", "<" at an even place and ">" at
E31E                             ; the odd one after it, and the handlers in the same order in memory.)
E31E   24 27        GCH:      DB   "$",27h   
E320   2D 2B 2E 2C 3C 3E 3F 40 5E 5F 21 GMINUS:   DB   "-","+",".",",","<",">","?","@","^","_","!"   
E32B   37           GLO:      DB   (g_hex-1) & 0FFh   
E32C   55                     DB   (g_nl-1) & 0FFh   
E32D   5F                     DB   (g_inc-1) & 0FFh   
E32E   5F                     DB   (g_inc-1) & 0FFh   
E32F   7B                     DB   (g_seed-1) & 0FFh   
E330   82                     DB   (g_rnd-1) & 0FFh   
E331   B9                     DB   (g_min-1) & 0FFh   
E332   B9                     DB   (g_min-1) & 0FFh   
E333   C8                     DB   (g_cur-1) & 0FFh   
E334   E5                     DB   (g_at-1) & 0FFh   
E335   0B                     DB   (g_cls-1) & 0FFh   
